From #32. The fix there establishes a rule that is not obvious from the code and
that a future maintainer would plausibly undo as a redundant copy:

  A scalar returned by XS code outside this distribution is normalised where it
  enters, not where it is used. decrypt_bytes returns "$plaintext" rather than
  $plaintext, and that stringify is load-bearing.

This is worth an ADR rather than only a code comment because it interacts
directly with ADR 0002. Since 0002 the type of a value is read off the SV
(_sv_kind reads SVf_IOK / SVf_NOK), so this distribution has made itself
sensitive to SV-level properties of scalars it did not create. 0002 records that
we trust the SV; nothing records that we must first make the SV trustworthy when
it came from foreign XS. The two belong next to each other.

Scope to decide in the ADR:
  - only decrypt_bytes (where a numeric read demonstrably happens), or every
    CryptX boundary including gcm_encrypt_authenticate ciphertext/tag and
    Crypt::PRNG::random_bytes? Those are only ever consumed length-aware today
    (base64, cipher input), so normalising them would be speculative -- but the
    rule reads cleaner if it is unconditional.
  - what the rule says about Crypt::Age, whose output (the data key) is likewise
    foreign XS but is never read numerically.

Not urgent, not blocking the #32 fix. Proposing rather than writing it: the
worker who found this did not have a mandate to add an ADR.
ADR written: docs/adr/0004-foreign-scalars-are-normalised-where-their-bytes-become-a-value.md (Status: accepted, matching 0001-0003).

SCOPE CHOSEN: narrow, and the rule is phrased as a precondition on READS rather
than on origins:

  A foreign scalar is normalised at the boundary where this distribution starts
  reading its bytes as a value. A foreign scalar that only ever passes through as
  opaque, length-delimited bytes is not normalised. A scalar whose SV state is
  itself the information is never normalised.

Today that names exactly one site, decrypt_bytes. No behavioural code change.

The scope question was settled by measurement, not by preference. Three findings:

1. The unconditional version of the rule is not merely speculative, it is WRONG.
   The parsers are foreign XS too, and a stringify normalisation measurably
   erases the flags ADR 0002 reads: YAML::XS bare 007 goes IOK|POK -> POK, i.e.
   type:int -> type:str. An unconditional rule would rewrite every numeric leaf
   in every document as a string. So the rule must carry a scope; the ADR puts
   the scope in the rule rather than in an unwritten exception list.

2. The ticket underestimated the other CryptX boundaries. Under a poisoned
   allocator (300 samples each): gcm_encrypt_authenticate ciphertext 299/300 NOT
   NUL-terminated, Crypt::PRNG::random_bytes 299/300 NOT terminated, hkdf and
   chacha20poly1305_decrypt_verify 300/300 NOT terminated. The GCM tag, hmac and
   both parsers are 0/300 -- CryptX splits by return shape, terminating its
   fixed-size digests and not its variable-length buffers. So they are affected;
   they are just unreachable, because every consumer is length-delimited.

3. Normalising them anyway was rejected on durability, not on cost: nothing can
   pin a stringify on the ciphertext, since no reader of it can fire the defect,
   so the next maintainer deletes it as a redundant copy and is right by every
   argument available at the site. decrypt_bytes is different in kind --
   t/18 goes red without it.

CRYPT::AGE: the data key falls on the opaque side, not normalised. The ADR
states explicitly that this does NOT rest on the measurement -- Crypt::Age
returns a terminated data key 0/300 only because decrypt_payload ends in
join(q{}, @chunks), while the chacha primitive under it is 300/300
non-terminated. The licence is that the data key is never interpreted here.

CODE: one non-behavioural change, in this lane -- a 7-line comment extension at
the decrypt_bytes stringify in lib/File/SOPS/Encrypted.pm pointing at ADR 0004
and saying it is deliberately the only such normalisation. That is the deletion
